-- anthropic-stock-below-ipo-price-20261125 (news; USA; verifiedAt NULL)
INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt", "verifiedAt", "authorName")
VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'anthropic-stock-below-ipo-price-20261125', 'news', 'USA',
'Anthropic stock falls below IPO price days after debut',
'Anthropic stock falls below IPO price days after debut',
'Anthropic stock closed below its $83 IPO price on Tuesday, just five days after the Claude maker''s record Nasdaq debut valued it above $2 trillion.',
'Anthropic stock closed below its $83 IPO price on Tuesday, just five days after the Claude maker''s record Nasdaq debut valued it above $2 trillion.',
$$<p>Anthropic stock was down below its $83 IPO price on Tuesday 24th November 2026, just five days after its record breaking Nasdaq debut, in a very sharp reversal which is the most definitive signal yet of the unease around AI valuations.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Event</td><td>Anthropic shares fall below IPO price</td></tr>
<tr><td>Date</td><td>24 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>IPO price</td><td>$83 a share</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tuesday low</td><td>$75.61 a share</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tuesday close</td><td>$81.91, down 6.5% on the day</td></tr>
<tr><td>Debut</td><td>Opened at $111.50 on 19 November 2026</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>Anthropic shares tumbled below their $83 initial public offering price in early trading on Tuesday, <a href="https://www.reuters.com/business/anthropic-breaks-below-83-ipo-price-debut-rally-unravels-2026-11-24/">Reuters</a> reported, extending a steep retreat from the $111.50 opening price on 19 November. <a href="https://www.marketwatch.com/story/anthropics-stock-just-broke-below-its-ipo-price-for-the-first-time-5dc82668">MarketWatch</a> reported that the stock traded as low as $75.61 before closing at $81.91, down 6.5% on the day, and that losses briefly pushed its market capitalisation to $999.5 billion, below the $1 trillion mark for the first time. The debut had been the largest IPO ever, raising $81.3 billion, with the fully diluted valuation briefly topping $2 trillion on the first day.</p>

<p>The fall came as the wider market was rallying on Monday on the back of Nvidia reinstating Jensen Huang. Anthropic missed out on Tuesday was a direct result of the concerns around stretched AI valuations and the sheer amount of money being thrown at computing infrastructure. <a href="https://www.fastcompany.com/91648273/ant-stock-is-down-why-anthropic-shares-dipped-below-ipo-price">Fast Company</a> pointed out that the fall was quite rapid in comparison to the other major tech IPO of the year, SpaceX, which took over a month to fall below its offer price. <a href="https://www.investopedia.com/anthropic-s-ipo-arrived-hot-then-the-stock-fell-below-its-ipo-price-ant-12276956">Investopedia</a> reported that Oppenheimer had started coverage with a bullish outperform rating and a $150 price target on Tuesday despite the shares being in freefall. Reuters reported that CEO Dario Amodei had sold over 3.37 million shares worth over $280m in a single day following the IPO, according to a filing late on Monday.</p>

<h2>What the Anthropic stock fall means for AI jobs</h2>

<p>A broken IPO matters beyond the shareholders. Anthropic is one of the biggest employers in the AI space. A valuation that has halved from its peak on IPO day in just five days has a direct impact on the company's ability to pay its staff in stock (which is how AI companies compete for talent) and it has a chilling effect on the funding climate for the sector as a whole. The wider issue is the one Reuters flagged up: the prospect of rising interest rates will add even more pressure to the cost of the computing build-out and if the AI market continues to deflate then the hiring boom in the data centres, the chips and the model labs will inevitably slow. The counterpoint is the analyst coverage: Oppenheimer have a $150 target which suggests that Wall Street still sees a much bigger company underneath the selloff. Growentix have been tracking Anthropic since its listing (including the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/anthropic-ipo-debut-ant-first-trade-20261119">IPO </a> and the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/anthropic-shares-fall-9-percent-second-day-ipo-20261122">second day fall</a>) and our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/usa">USA jobs</a> hub is tracking AI hiring as the market has moved.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>The sources don't agree on the precise closing price at all (MarketWatch and Investopedia have it at $81.91 down 6.5%, others have it at around $82 or $81.6 at various points during the day). They don't say why Amodei sold shares the day after the debut, merely that the sale was disclosed in a filing. There's no suggestion in the sources that Anthropic is going to scale back hiring at all and they don't suggest the entire AI sector is in a bubble (just that valuations are under pressure).</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.reuters.com/business/anthropic-breaks-below-83-ipo-price-debut-rally-unravels-2026-11-24/">Reuters</a> for the break below the IPO price and the Amodei share sale; <a href="https://www.marketwatch.com/story/anthropics-stock-just-broke-below-its-ipo-price-for-the-first-time-5dc82668">MarketWatch</a> for the session low, close and market cap; <a href="https://www.investopedia.com/anthropic-s-ipo-arrived-hot-then-the-stock-fell-below-its-ipo-price-ant-12276956">Investopedia</a> for the close and the Oppenheimer rating; <a href="https://www.fastcompany.com/91648273/ant-stock-is-down-why-anthropic-shares-dipped-below-ipo-price">Fast Company</a> for the comparison with the SpaceX IPO.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>What was Anthropic's IPO price?</h3>
<p>Anthropic priced its shares at $83. They opened at $111.50 on the 19th of November and hit $115.10 on the first day before coming back down.</p>
<h3>How much has Anthropic stock dropped since launch?</h3>
<p>The fall from the $111.50 opening to Tuesday's close of $81.91 is some 27% and the market value briefly dipped below $1trillion.</p>
$$,
$$<p>Anthropic stock was down below its $83 IPO price on Tuesday 24th November 2026, just five days after its record breaking Nasdaq debut, in a very sharp reversal which is the most definitive signal yet of the unease around AI valuations.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Event</td><td>Anthropic shares fall below IPO price</td></tr>
<tr><td>Date</td><td>24 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>IPO price</td><td>$83 a share</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tuesday low</td><td>$75.61 a share</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tuesday close</td><td>$81.91, down 6.5% on the day</td></tr>
<tr><td>Debut</td><td>Opened at $111.50 on 19 November 2026</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>Anthropic shares tumbled below their $83 initial public offering price in early trading on Tuesday, <a href="https://www.reuters.com/business/anthropic-breaks-below-83-ipo-price-debut-rally-unravels-2026-11-24/">Reuters</a> reported, extending a steep retreat from the $111.50 opening price on 19 November. <a href="https://www.marketwatch.com/story/anthropics-stock-just-broke-below-its-ipo-price-for-the-first-time-5dc82668">MarketWatch</a> reported that the stock traded as low as $75.61 before closing at $81.91, down 6.5% on the day, and that losses briefly pushed its market capitalisation to $999.5 billion, below the $1 trillion mark for the first time. The debut had been the largest IPO ever, raising $81.3 billion, with the fully diluted valuation briefly topping $2 trillion on the first day.</p>

<p>The fall came as the wider market was rallying on Monday on the back of Nvidia reinstating Jensen Huang. Anthropic missed out on Tuesday was a direct result of the concerns around stretched AI valuations and the sheer amount of money being thrown at computing infrastructure. <a href="https://www.fastcompany.com/91648273/ant-stock-is-down-why-anthropic-shares-dipped-below-ipo-price">Fast Company</a> pointed out that the fall was quite rapid in comparison to the other major tech IPO of the year, SpaceX, which took over a month to fall below its offer price. <a href="https://www.investopedia.com/anthropic-s-ipo-arrived-hot-then-the-stock-fell-below-its-ipo-price-ant-12276956">Investopedia</a> reported that Oppenheimer had started coverage with a bullish outperform rating and a $150 price target on Tuesday despite the shares being in freefall. Reuters reported that CEO Dario Amodei had sold over 3.37 million shares worth over $280m in a single day following the IPO, according to a filing late on Monday.</p>

<h2>What the Anthropic stock fall means for AI jobs</h2>

<p>A broken IPO matters beyond the shareholders. Anthropic is one of the biggest employers in the AI space. A valuation that has halved from its peak on IPO day in just five days has a direct impact on the company's ability to pay its staff in stock (which is how AI companies compete for talent) and it has a chilling effect on the funding climate for the sector as a whole. The wider issue is the one Reuters flagged up: the prospect of rising interest rates will add even more pressure to the cost of the computing build-out and if the AI market continues to deflate then the hiring boom in the data centres, the chips and the model labs will inevitably slow. The counterpoint is the analyst coverage: Oppenheimer have a $150 target which suggests that Wall Street still sees a much bigger company underneath the selloff. Growentix have been tracking Anthropic since its listing (including the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/anthropic-ipo-debut-ant-first-trade-20261119">IPO </a> and the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/anthropic-shares-fall-9-percent-second-day-ipo-20261122">second day fall</a>) and our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/usa">USA jobs</a> hub is tracking AI hiring as the market has moved.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>The sources don't agree on the precise closing price at all (MarketWatch and Investopedia have it at $81.91 down 6.5%, others have it at around $82 or $81.6 at various points during the day). They don't say why Amodei sold shares the day after the debut, merely that the sale was disclosed in a filing. There's no suggestion in the sources that Anthropic is going to scale back hiring at all and they don't suggest the entire AI sector is in a bubble (just that valuations are under pressure).</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.reuters.com/business/anthropic-breaks-below-83-ipo-price-debut-rally-unravels-2026-11-24/">Reuters</a> for the break below the IPO price and the Amodei share sale; <a href="https://www.marketwatch.com/story/anthropics-stock-just-broke-below-its-ipo-price-for-the-first-time-5dc82668">MarketWatch</a> for the session low, close and market cap; <a href="https://www.investopedia.com/anthropic-s-ipo-arrived-hot-then-the-stock-fell-below-its-ipo-price-ant-12276956">Investopedia</a> for the close and the Oppenheimer rating; <a href="https://www.fastcompany.com/91648273/ant-stock-is-down-why-anthropic-shares-dipped-below-ipo-price">Fast Company</a> for the comparison with the SpaceX IPO.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>What was Anthropic's IPO price?</h3>
<p>Anthropic priced its shares at $83. They opened at $111.50 on the 19th of November and hit $115.10 on the first day before coming back down.</p>
<h3>How much has Anthropic stock dropped since launch?</h3>
<p>The fall from the $111.50 opening to Tuesday's close of $81.91 is some 27% and the market value briefly dipped below $1trillion.</p>
