<tr><td>Full-year guidance</td><td>$202.0 billion, raised from $192.0 billion</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>Dell reported adjusted earnings of $9.45 a share (vs $6.60 expected) according to <a href="https://www.investors.com/news/technology/dell-stock-fiscal-q3-2027-earnings/">Investor's Business Daily</a>, and revenue of $54.94bn (vs about $49.3bn expected) as reported by IBD. <a href="https://www.zacks.com/stock/news/3034852/dell-technologies-dell-q3-earnings-how-key-metrics-compare-to-wall-street-estimates/">Zacks</a> had the revenue surprise at 8.98% above its consensus of $50.41bn. It was the fastest quarterly revenue growth in the company in more than three decades, according to IBD.</p>

<p>Almost all of the growth was from Infrastructure Solutions Group (servers, networking and storage) which saw revenue triple to $42.0bn (up 198%). AI-optimised server revenue was up 291% to $22.0bn, traditional servers and networking up 235% to $15.0bn. Client Solutions Group (PCs) grew just 3% to $12.9bn with commercial sales up 6% to $11.3bn and consumer sales down 15% to $1.59bn. Dell has raised its full year revenue guidance by $10bn to $202bn and its AI server guidance to $78bn from $74bn. For the quarter it guided revenue to $56.3 billion and adjusted earnings to $8.40 a share. <a href="https://www.morningstar.com/news/dow-jones/202611247687/dell-lifts-outlook-as-3q-revenue-doubles-on-server-demand-adds-25-billion-to-buyback-plan">Morningstar</a> noted that Dell added $25bn to its share buyback plan and returned a record $4.9bn to shareholders in the quarter.</p>

<h2>What the Dell earnings beat means for AI jobs</h2>

<p>The numbers put Dell back at the heart of the AI build-out after a week in which the entire AI sector was rattled by the Nvidia leadership crisis. A company whose server business has tripled in a year is in the midst of a major hiring and expansion drive in data centre engineering, sales and manufacturing. Its order book is a very good barometer for the suppliers and contractors in the space. The contrast within the company is also a jobs signal in that the infrastructure side is doing very well and the consumer PC side is contracting. The hiring is therefore in the enterprise and AI infrastructure space, not in consumer hardware. Growentix have been tracking the AI earnings season very closely indeed, from the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-stock-selloff-sp-500-correction-20261124">AI stock selloff through to the S&P 500 correction</a> and our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/usa">USA jobs</a> hub has been watching how US tech hiring has responded to those results.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>The sources do not say how much of the AI server revenue is profit rather than pass-through hardware cost, and Dell did not break out margins for the AI server line in the release. One estimate differs slightly across outlets: FactSet expectations cited by Investor's Business Daily were $6.60 adjusted earnings and $49.32 billion revenue, while Zacks put its consensus at $6.59 and $50.41 billion. The sources also do not say whether Dell will add headcount to match the server growth, or how much of the raised full-year forecast depends on a small number of large AI customers.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.dell.com/en-us/newsroom/announcements/dell-technologies-delivers-third-quarter-fiscal-2027-financial-results">Dell</a> for the official results and guidance; <a href="https://www.investors.com/news/technology/dell-stock-fiscal-q3-2027-earnings/">Investor's Business Daily</a> for the FactSet comparison and the share reaction; <a href="https://www.morningstar.com/news/dow-jones/202611247687/dell-lifts-outlook-as-3q-revenue-doubles-on-server-demand-adds-25-billion-to-buyback-plan">Morningstar</a> for the buyback addition and the profit figures; <a href="https://www.zacks.com/stock/news/3034852/dell-technologies-dell-q3-earnings-how-key-metrics-compare-to-wall-street-estimates">Zacks</a> for the segment by segment comparison with the estimates.</p>

