VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'dow-jones-50000-nasdaq-falls-20261125', 'news', 'USA',
'Dow Jones tops 50,000 as Nasdaq falls in tech selloff',
'Dow Jones tops 50,000 as Nasdaq falls in tech selloff',
'The Dow Jones rose above 50,000 on Tuesday while the Nasdaq fell 1.48%, as investors rotated out of tech a day after the Nvidia-driven rally.',
'The Dow Jones rose above 50,000 on Tuesday while the Nasdaq fell 1.48%, as investors rotated out of tech a day after the Nvidia-driven rally.',
$$<p>The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose above 50,000 on Tuesday 24th November 2026, the Nasdaq down 1.48% as investors rotated out of the tech stocks the day after the Nvidia driven rally.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Event</td><td>US stock market, Tuesday 24 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dow Jones</td><td>Up 0.85%, above 50,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>S and P 500</td><td>Down 0.48% to about 7,201</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nasdaq Composite</td><td>Down 1.48% to 23,733.54</td></tr>
<tr><td>Monday 23 Nov</td><td>Nasdaq up 6.51%, S and P up 4.73%</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>The Dow closed up for a second day in a row on Tuesday, breaking above the 50,000 threshold for the first time in just about a week according to <a href="https://www.marketwatch.com/story/live-coverage-stock-market-today-dow-sp-500-nasdaq-extend-gains-jensen-huang-ceo-return-nvidia-dell-results/card/dow-logs-back-to-back-gains-but-nasdaq-and-s-p-500-end-lower-as-tech-selloff-resumes-iMz9x8Tbk8OK1yqBXKIV">MarketWatch</a>. There was a clear rotation into the defensive and out of the tech sector which saw the S&P 500 and Nasdaq both down after Monday's very strong but very short-lived rally which had been triggered by the reinstatement of Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang. Semiconductors and memory stocks were the worst hit on the day and volatility was up ahead of the Thanksgiving holiday on Thursday. The Russell 2000 climbed 0.6%, and medicals, retailers, payments, exchanges and cybersecurity all did well, <a href="https://www.investors.com/market-trend/stock-market-today/dow-jones-futures-nvidia-anthropic-ai-retreat-fed-inflation-data/">Investor's Business Daily</a> reports.</p>

<p>The split was quite sharp. <a href="https://global.newspim.com/news/view/20261125000281">NewsPim</a> had the Dow up 423.39 (0.85%) at 50,018.70, the S&P 500 down 36.08 (0.50%) at 7,201.09 and the Nasdaq down 356.02 (1.48%) at 23,733.54. The new US tariff plans were also a drag on the markets with the market absorbing the news that the Trump administration is now in the process of replacing the tariffs that the Supreme Court has struck down. Money rotated rather than simply left with health care, staples, financials and real estate all in the lead, technology alone was down. Wednesday will see the week's big data (core PCE inflation and revised GDP) before the markets close for Thanksgiving.</p>

<h2>What the Dow Jones move means for jobs</h2>

<p>A Dow above 50,000 with the Nasdaq falling is a rotation, not a crash, and the jobs signal follows the money. Healthcare, staples and financials hiring stays supported while chip and AI hiring cools, at least until the data lands on Wednesday. The two-day swing also shows how much of the market now hangs on one story: Monday's 6.51% Nasdaq jump was the Nvidia reinstatement, and Tuesday's fall was the same trade unwinding plus tariff fears. For workers in tech, the message is to watch Wednesday's inflation print — a hot number strengthens the case for higher rates, which is what the tech selloff is pricing. Growentix tracks the market through our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-stock-selloff-sp-500-correction-20261124">AI selloff coverage</a> and the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/usa">USA jobs</a> hub, which follows how market swings feed into hiring.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>The close figures vary slightly from outlet to outlet. NewsPim has the Dow at 50,018.70 (up 423.39) whereas another wire has it at 50,028.79 (up 433.48) and the S&P at 7,202.45 rather than 7,201.09. The difference is minimal and again is probably just a matter of different closing points. No one source gives us a definitive close. The sources don't say whether the rotation will continue past Wednesday's data or whether the Dow's move above 50,000 will be sustained through the holiday shortened week.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.marketwatch.com/livecoverage/stock-market-today-dow-s-p-500-nasdaq-extend-gains-jensen-huang-ceo-return-nvidia-dell-results/card/dow-logs-back-to-back-gains-but-nasdaq-and-s-p-500-end-lower-as-tech-selloff-resumes-iMz9x8Tbk8OK1yqBXKIV">MarketWatch</a> for the Dow's two days in a row gains and the rotation; <a href="https://www.investors.com/market-trend/stock-market-today/dow-jones-futures-nvidia-anthropic-ai-retreat-fed-inflation-data/">Investor's Business Daily</a> for the sector split and the Russell 2000; <a href="https://global.newspim.com/news/view/20261125000281">NewsPim</a> for the close.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Did the Dow close above 50,000?</h3>
<p>Yes. The Dow up 0.85% on Tuesday to above 50,000, its second day in a row, the Nasdaq down 1.48%.</p>
<h3>Why did the Nasdaq fall after Monday's rally?</h3>
<p>Investors rotated out of tech and semis into the defensive sectors and the reports of new US tariffs added to the selling pressure.</p>
