<tr><td>Fall in November</td><td>8.87% so far this month</td></tr>
<tr><td>Record close</td><td>$5,318.40 on 29 January 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>Distance from record</td><td>33% below the all-time high</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>Front-month gold dropped 2.4% in New York to $3,559.90 a troy ounce on Tuesday according to the <a href="https://www.wsj.com/finance/commodities-futures/gold-rises-amid-risk-on-sentiment-across-markets-ae66946a">Wall Street Journal</a>, reversing a good chunk of the Nvidia-driven gains of the previous session as the market re-focused on inflation and the outlook for Fed rates. The settlement was the second lowest of the year (33.06% below the record close of $5,318.40 set on 29 January 2026) and 17.70% lower year to date. It had jumped 3.87% on Monday to $3,651.40, its best performance of the month, before the sharp reversal on Tuesday.</p>

<p>November has been terrible for gold. <a href="https://www.xauusdglobal.com/gold-price-history/2026/11/">XAUUSD Global</a>(using COMEX data) has gold down 8.87% in November to date, from $3,898.90 at the end of October to $3,553.00. That's the worst November since 2000. The low point of the month was $3,515.40 on 20th November, the lowest since 28th August 2025. Two things went up as gold went down from its January high: the 10 year real yield rose from 1.89% to 2.79% by 23rd November and expectations of further Fed rate hikes have strengthened. Higher real yields make gold (which pays no yield) less attractive.</p>

<h2>What the gold price fall means for jobs</h2>

<p>Gold's fall is a reflection of the rate outlook and the rate outlook is what drives hiring everywhere. When the markets are pricing in higher US rates, it becomes more expensive for employers to borrow, mortgages become more expensive and the gold price falls. All three move together. For the workers in Nepal and India where gold is both a form of savings and the metal used for weddings, the fall in price has an impact at home as well. The price of the metal itself is lower. For the miners it's a mixed picture. Prices are still very much above where they were a few years ago so mines are still hiring, but the 33% fall from the record has certainly dampened the enthusiasm. The next one is Wednesday's US PCE inflation report which could well push rates (and gold) either way. Growentix tracks the data through our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-stock-selloff-sp-500-correction-20261124">market coverage</a> and the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles">News hub</a>.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>One figure is slightly different actually: the Wall Street Journal put Tuesday's COMEX settlement at $3,559.90, while MKViz, using Yahoo Finance data, gives $3,543 for the same day (different contracts/feeds I suppose). The sources don't actually say where gold goes next: the PCE report on Wednesday or the Fed's December meeting that will decide it. They don't break out at all how much of the November fall was due to investors selling versus central banks.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.wsj.com/finance/commodities-futures/gold-rises-amid-risk-on-sentiment-across-markets-ae66946a">Wall Street Journal</a> for the Tuesday settlement and the record comparison; <a href="https://www.xauusdglobal.com/gold-price-history/2026/11/">XAUUSD Global</a> for the November daily prices and the worst November ranking; <a href="https://mkviz.com/gold-price-history/">MKViz</a> for the longer history and the real yield explanation.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>How much has the gold price fallen in November?</h3>
<p>Gold is down 8.87% in November to date (from $3,898.90 at the end of October) making it the worst November since 2000.</p>
<h3>Why is the gold price going down?</h3>
<p>Expectations of higher US rates have pushed real yields up, making gold (which pays no interest) less attractive to hold.</p>
