(gen_random_uuid(), 'hp-earnings-q4-2026-20261125', 'news', 'USA',
'HP earnings beat forecasts as AI PCs hit 60% of shipments',
'HP earnings beat forecasts as AI PCs hit 60% of shipments',
'HP earnings beat forecasts with fourth-quarter revenue of $16.0 billion, as AI PCs reached nearly 60% of shipments and the dividend rose.',
'HP earnings beat forecasts with fourth-quarter revenue of $16.0 billion, as AI PCs reached nearly 60% of shipments and the dividend rose.',
$$<p>HP earnings beat Wall Street expectations on Tuesday 24th November 2026 with the computer maker reported fourth-quarter revenue of $16.0bn (up 9.3% year on year) and raised its quarterly dividend.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Event</td><td>HP fiscal 2026 fourth-quarter and full-year results</td></tr>
<tr><td>Date</td><td>24 November 2026, after the US market close</td></tr>
<tr><td>Q4 revenue</td><td>$16.0 billion, up 9.3% year on year</td></tr>
<tr><td>Q4 non-GAAP EPS</td><td>$0.83, against a $0.74 consensus estimate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Full-year revenue</td><td>$60.5 billion, up 9.5% year on year</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dividend</td><td>Raised to $0.31 a share, payable 6 January 2027</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>HP reported quarterly earnings of $0.83 a share on a non-GAAP basis, beating the Zacks consensus estimate of $0.74, while revenue of $16 billion beat the consensus by 6.13%, <a href="https://www.zacks.com/stock/news/3034826/hp-hpq-tops-q4-earnings-and-revenue-estimates">Zacks</a> reported. On a GAAP basis, fourth-quarter earnings were $0.84 a share, flat year over year, and net earnings were $769 million versus $795 million a year earlier. For the full fiscal year, revenue rose 9.5% to $60.5 billion and non-GAAP earnings were $3.33 a share, up 6.7%, while GAAP earnings slipped 1.1% to $2.62. Both the quarterly and full-year figures included a $0.26 favourable impact from tariff refunds, <a href="https://www.hp.com/us-en/newsroom/press-releases/2026/hp-inc-reports-fiscal-2026-full-year-and-fourth-quarter-results.html">HP</a> said.</p>

<p>The split between the two divisions tells the story. Personal Systems revenue was up 14% to $11.8bn and AI PCs were nearly 60% of the shipments, said interim CEO Bruce Broussard said via <a href="https://www.tradingview.com/news/benzinga:2cbe2b9aa094b:0-hp-posts-strong-q4-earnings-with-ai-pcs-nearly-60-of-shipments/">Benzinga</a>. Printing revenue was down 1% to $4.3bn. HP generated $4.0bn in operating cash flow and $3.3bn in free cash flow for the year and returned $2.3bn to shareholders. Looking ahead, HP guided fiscal 2027 non-GAAP EPS to $3.15-$3.45 and free cash flow of $2.9bn-$3.1bn with first quarter EPS of $0.66-$0.76.</p>

<h2>What the HP earnings beat means for tech jobs</h2>

<p>The AI PC is now the majority of what HP ships and that has a huge impact on the workers in the PC supply chain. Hiring is clearly shifting to AI capable devices, on device software and the commercial customers that are buying them. The print business is still under pressure. HP also called out memory and storage cost pressures which it's managing through pricing. That's a constant reminder that hardware margins and the jobs that go with them are as much about component cost as they are about demand. The dividend increase and the very cautious 2027 outlook together point to a company that is choosing to reward shareholders while being very cautious about hiring into an uncertain year. Anyone following the sector can go and compare that to the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/dell-earnings-november-24-preview-ai-backlog-20261123">Dell earnings preview</a> and the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-stock-selloff-sp-500-correction-20261124">AI stock selloff</a>. Our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/usa">USA jobs</a> hub is tracking US tech hiring as these results come in.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>The sources don't give us the number of AI PC units shipped by HP (only that they were nearly 60% of the total), so we can't work out the absolute figure from the release. They also don't tell us how much of the Personal Systems growth was due to price increases rather than volume. HP has previously stated that pricing actions were taken to deal with higher commodity costs. The sources don't indicate whether the interim CEO will be made permanent or whether HP has any plans to add or reduce headcount in fiscal 2027.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.hp.com/us-en/newsroom/press-releases/2026/hp-inc-reports-fiscal-2026-full-year-and-fourth-quarter-results.html">HP</a> for the official results, guidance and dividend; <a href="https://www.zacks.com/stock/news/3034826/hp-hpq-tops-q4-earnings-and-revenue-estimates">Zacks</a> for the comparison to consensus estimates; <a href="https://www.tradingview.com/news/benzinga:2cbe2b9aa094b:0-hp-posts-strong-q4-earnings-with-ai-pcs-nearly-60-of-shipments/">Benzinga</a> for the AI PC share of shipments and the comments of the interim CEO; <a href="https://www.tradingview.com/news/dpa_afx:cf1e87e3fbdb3:0-hp-reports-q4-profit-decline-despite-revenue-growth/">dpa-AFX</a> for the GAAP profit decline.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Did HP beat earnings forecasts?</h3>
<p>Yes. HP reported non-GAAP earnings of $0.83 a share vs a $0.74 consensus and revenue of $16.0 billion vs a consensus of around $15.5 billion.</p>
<h3>What's HP's dividend at the moment?</h3>
<p>The board has increased the quarterly dividend to $0.31 a share, payable on 6th January 2027 to shareholders of record on 9th December 2026.</p>
