-- huawei-guilty-trade-secrets-verdict-20261125 (news; China; verifiedAt NULL)
INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt", "verifiedAt", "authorName")
VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'huawei-guilty-trade-secrets-verdict-20261125', 'news', 'China',
'Huawei guilty of stealing trade secrets, US jury finds',
'Huawei guilty of stealing trade secrets, US jury finds',
'A Brooklyn jury found Huawei guilty of bank fraud, sanctions violations and trade-secret theft, convicting the Chinese tech giant on 10 of 12 counts.',
'A Brooklyn jury found Huawei guilty of bank fraud, sanctions violations and trade-secret theft, convicting the Chinese tech giant on 10 of 12 counts.',
$$<p>A federal jury in Brooklyn found Huawei guilty of bank fraud, sanctions violations and conspiracy to commit trade secret theft on Tuesday 24th November, convicting the Chinese telecoms giant on 10 of the 12 criminal counts it was charged with after a trial which lasted over two months.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Company</td><td>Huawei Technologies and three subsidiaries</td></tr>
<tr><td>Verdict</td><td>Guilty on 10 of 12 counts</td></tr>
<tr><td>Court</td><td>US District Court, Brooklyn, New York</td></tr>
<tr><td>Judge</td><td>Ann M. Donnelly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Trial</td><td>12 weeks; more than 60 prosecution witnesses, 15 for the defence</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sentencing</td><td>14 September 2027</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>The jury found Huawei and three of its subsidiaries (Huawei Device, Futurewei Technologies and Huawei Device USA) guilty of conspiracy to commit trade secret theft, wire fraud, bank fraud, conspiracy to defraud the United States, violations of the International Emergency Economic Powers Act and money laundering conspiracy, according to the <a href="https://www.justice.gov/usao-edny/pr/chinese-telecommunications-conglomerate-huawei-and-subsidiaries-convicted-sanctions">US Justice Department</a>. The jury found the company not guilty of the two most serious counts: racketeering conspiracy (which alleged Huawei was a criminal enterprise) and conspiracy to obstruct justice.</p>

<p>The case started in 2018 with US prosecutors alleging that Huawei had misled HSBC, Standard Chartered and BNP Paribas about its business in Iran. It had been set up so that Skycom Tech (a Huawei subsidiary) would appear to be a separate entity, thereby allowing money to flow in and out of Iran in breach of US sanctions. New charges were added in 2020 alleging Huawei of conspiring to steal trade secrets from US tech firms. The US prosecutors told the jury that Huawei had stolen router source code from Cisco, antenna technology from Quintel and had hired a professor to get access to a circuit board made by CNEX. <a href="https://www.reuters.com/legal/litigation/chinas-huawei-convicted-us-criminal-trial-stemming-iran-business-2026-11-24/">Reuters</a> reported this.</p>

<p>The trial went on for 12 weeks with over 60 prosecution witnesses and the defence calling 15. Jurors began deliberating on the afternoon of 18th November and took five days to reach a verdict. The case came to light in December 2018 when Meng Wanzhou (Huawei's CFO and daughter of the company's founder) was arrested in Canada on a US warrant, which triggered a major diplomatic row between the US, China and Canada. The prosecutors showed the jury a statement that was part of Meng's deferred prosecution agreement in which she admitted that Huawei had engaged in business in Iran.</p>

<p>Huawei's defence argued that the banks knew about its Iran business and were competing for it, and that the trade-secret allegations were the work of a rogue employee rather than company policy. Lead defence lawyer Brian Heberlig called the acquittal on the racketeering count a partial win, saying it "repudiated the central government narrative in the case", according to <a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/amp/economy/2026/11/24/chinese-tech-giant-huawei-found-guilty-of-conspiracy-to-steal-trade-secrets">Al Jazeera</a>. "Huawei's success did not come from theft, racketeering or fraud," he said. "It came from the same hard work that guided Mr Ren."</p>

<h2>What the Huawei guilty verdict means</h2>

<p>The verdict concludes a nearly seven year criminal case which has run alongside one of the world's largest telecoms equipment makers. It comes just days after President Trump met with President Xi in Shenzhen, a potent reminder that the US-China tech battle is being fought in the courtroom as much as in the trade talks. For Huawei the immediate impact is financial: when sentenced on 14 September 2027, the company faces criminal fines, forfeiture and restitution, though the size of the penalties is not yet known. The verdict also strengthens the case for the 2019 US trade blacklist which effectively cut Huawei off from US technology, the blacklist having been imposed the year after the indictment.</p>

<p>The acquittal on the racketeering count matters too. That was the only count which actually linked all the allegations together into a single criminal enterprise and its dismissal severely restricts the extent to which the prosecutors can characterise Huawei's activities as organised crime. The guilty verdicts on fraud, sanctions evasion and trade secret theft remain however as a formal finding that at least some of the company's ascent was achieved through deception (in the words of the US Attorney for the Eastern District of New York). For the wider industry it serves as a stark warning that US sanctions and trade secret laws extend well beyond the US borders. More on the trade tensions which underpinned the case is in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/trump-tariffs-china-eu-20261125">Trump tariffs plan for China and the EU</a> and the latest from around the world is in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles">News</a> section.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>None of the sources say what the financial penalties will be, or whether the sentencing judge will impose any further restrictions on Huawei's business other than fines and forfeiture. There is no information as to what happens to Huawei's US operations or its employees as a result of this. There is no mention of any reaction from the Chinese government or from founder Ren Zhengfei. The sources don't say whether the prosecutors will be appealing the acquittals on the racketeering and obstruction counts, nor do they say whether the verdict will have any impact on the separate US restrictions on Huawei's access to US chips and software.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.justice.gov/usao-edny/pr/chinese-telecommunications-conglomerate-huawei-and-subsidiaries-convicted-sanctions"> US Justice Department</a> for the verdict, the counts and the penalties at sentencing; <a href="https://www.reuters.com/legal/litigation/chinas-huawei-convicted-us-criminal-trial-stemming-iran-business-2026-11-24/">Reuters</a> for the trade secret allegations and trial details; <a href="https://www.cnn.com/2026/11/24/china/china-huawei-iran-conviction-intl-hnk?cid=external-feeds_iluminar_meta">CNN</a> for the sentencing date and the case history; <a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/amp/economy/2026/11/24/chinese-tech-giant-huawei-found-guilty-of-conspiracy-to-steal-trade-secrets">Al Jazeera</a> for the defence reaction and the Meng Wanzhou background.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>What was Huawei found guilty of?</h3>
<p>A Brooklyn jury found Huawei guilty on 10 of 12 counts, including bank fraud, wire fraud, sanctions violations, money laundering conspiracy and conspiracy to steal trade secrets. It was acquitted of racketeering conspiracy and conspiracy to obstruct justice.</p>

<h3>What's next for Huawei?</h3>
<p>Sentencing is due 14th September 2027, when the company faces criminal fines, forfeiture and restitution). The motions and responses ahead of sentencing are scheduled to start in the third week of January.</p>
