<h2>What the Huawei guilty verdict means</h2>

<p>The verdict concludes a nearly seven year criminal case which has run alongside one of the world's largest telecoms equipment makers. It comes just days after President Trump met with President Xi in Shenzhen, a potent reminder that the US-China tech battle is being fought in the courtroom as much as in the trade talks. For Huawei the immediate impact is financial: when sentenced on 14 September 2027, the company faces criminal fines, forfeiture and restitution, though the size of the penalties is not yet known. The verdict also strengthens the case for the 2019 US trade blacklist which effectively cut Huawei off from US technology, the blacklist having been imposed the year after the indictment.</p>

<p>The acquittal on the racketeering count matters too. That was the only count which actually linked all the allegations together into a single criminal enterprise and its dismissal severely restricts the extent to which the prosecutors can characterise Huawei's activities as organised crime. The guilty verdicts on fraud, sanctions evasion and trade secret theft remain however as a formal finding that at least some of the company's ascent was achieved through deception (in the words of the US Attorney for the Eastern District of New York). For the wider industry it serves as a stark warning that US sanctions and trade secret laws extend well beyond the US borders. More on the trade tensions which underpinned the case is in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/trump-tariffs-china-eu-20261125">Trump tariffs plan for China and the EU</a> and the latest from around the world is in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles">News</a> section.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>None of the sources say what the financial penalties will be, or whether the sentencing judge will impose any further restrictions on Huawei's business other than fines and forfeiture. There is no information as to what happens to Huawei's US operations or its employees as a result of this. There is no mention of any reaction from the Chinese government or from founder Ren Zhengfei. The sources don't say whether the prosecutors will be appealing the acquittals on the racketeering and obstruction counts, nor do they say whether the verdict will have any impact on the separate US restrictions on Huawei's access to US chips and software.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.justice.gov/usao-edny/pr/chinese-telecommunications-conglomerate-huawei-and-subsidiaries-convicted-sanctions"> US Justice Department</a> for the verdict, the counts and the penalties at sentencing; <a href="https://www.reuters.com/legal/litigation/chinas-huawei-convicted-us-criminal-trial-stemming-iran-business-2026-11-24/">Reuters</a> for the trade secret allegations and trial details; <a href="https://www.cnn.com/2026/11/24/china/china-huawei-iran-conviction-intl-hnk?cid=external-feeds_iluminar_meta">CNN</a> for the sentencing date and the case history; <a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/amp/economy/2026/11/24/chinese-tech-giant-huawei-found-guilty-of-conspiracy-to-steal-trade-secrets">Al Jazeera</a> for the defence reaction and the Meng Wanzhou background.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>What was Huawei found guilty of?</h3>
<p>A Brooklyn jury found Huawei guilty on 10 of 12 counts, including bank fraud, wire fraud, sanctions violations, money laundering conspiracy and conspiracy to steal trade secrets. It was acquitted of racketeering conspiracy and conspiracy to obstruct justice.</p>

<h3>What's next for Huawei?</h3>
<p>Sentencing is due 14th September 2027, when the company faces criminal fines, forfeiture and restitution). The motions and responses ahead of sentencing are scheduled to start in the third week of January.</p>
