-- india-gdp-growth-q2-preview-20261125 (news; India; verifiedAt NULL)
INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt", "verifiedAt", "authorName")
VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'india-gdp-growth-q2-preview-20261125', 'news', 'India',
'India GDP growth forecast at 7.8% as data release nears',
'India GDP growth forecast at 7.8% as data release nears',
'India GDP growth is forecast at about 7.8% for the July-September quarter, with SBI Research and Bank of Baroda split on the exact figure.',
'India GDP growth is forecast at about 7.8% for the July-September quarter, with SBI Research and Bank of Baroda split on the exact figure.',
$$<p>India GDP growth is expected to be around 7.8% for the July-September quarter, according to SBI Research as the country is due to release its official second-quarter growth number later this month.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Event</td><td>India Q2 FY27 GDP estimate, due later in November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>SBI Research forecast</td><td>About 7.8% year on year</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bank of Baroda forecast</td><td>7.4% to 7.5%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Previous quarter</td><td>7.8% in Q1 FY27</td></tr>
<tr><td>Full-year forecast</td><td>About 7.3% in FY27, per the EAC-PM chairman</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What the forecasts say</h2>

<p>India's GDP growth is estimated at around 7.8% in the second quarter of the financial year 2026-27, while gross value added growth is expected to be in the 8%-8.2% range, according to an SBI Research report (as per the <a href="https://cfo.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/economy/indias-q2-fy27-gdp-growth-pegged-at-7-8-gva-growth-seen-at-8-8-2-sbi-research/135732270?utm_source=top_news&utm_medium=sectionListing">Economic Times</a>). SBI's model (using 54 high frequency indicators across agriculture, industry and services) has nominal GDP growth at 11% to 11.5% for the quarter. <a href="https://bankofbaroda.bank.in/-/media/project/bob/countrywebsites/india/economic-scenario/data-releases/2026/bob-economics-q2fy27-gdp-preview-16-15.pdf">Bank of Baroda</a> is more conservative at 7.4%-7.5% (vs 8.1% in Q2 FY25-26), with agriculture slowing to 2.8% from 5.1% following a south-west monsoon 12.6% below the long period average.</p>

<p>The wider outlook stays strong. S Mahendra Dev, chairman of the Economic Advisory Council to the Prime Minister, expects India's economy to grow 7.3% in the current financial year and around 7.7% next quarter, <a href="https://www.moneycontrol.com/news/business/economy/india-s-gdp-growth-pegged-at-7-3-in-fy26-27-may-touch-7-7-next-quarter-eac-pm-chairman-14082764.html">Moneycontrol</a> reported, and said the country was targeting $1 trillion in exports in FY27. Bank of Baroda projects 7.2% for the full year with an upward bias, while BofA Global Research expects 7.7% in the October-November period. Services remain the engine: the services PMI rose to a four-month high of 55.8 in October, and listed companies' earnings grew strongly, with profits of about 2,300 listed firms up 33% year on year.</p>

<h2>What India GDP growth means for jobs</h2>

<p>Growth near 8% is why India remains one of the world's biggest job creators, but the split inside the numbers matters for jobseekers. Manufacturing is expected to grow around 12% on strong industrial activity, which supports factory and engineering hiring. At the same time, agriculture faces a weak monsoon and El Nino, with the government expecting 11.4% lower production this year — bad news for rural work. The export target of $1 trillion also matters: sustained export momentum and diversifying markets are the official reasons for confidence, and export-facing sectors from IT to manufacturing are where the new jobs are most likely to appear. The risk to watch is the tariff probe in Washington, which names India among 16 economies facing possible new US duties. Growentix follows India through our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/balen-shah-modi-talks-outcome-nepal-india-20261124">Nepal-India coverage</a> and the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/countries">country's hub</a>, which tracks hiring market by market.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>No source has the official figure as the Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation has not yet released it — these are all projections. SBI's figure is 7.8% and Bank of Baroda's is 7.4%-7.5% (a difference of up to 0.4pp). The sources don't say when in November the release will actually be. They don't break the projections down by employment so no employment figure can be derived from them and they don't indicate how much of the growth is going to the workers in the non-metropolitan areas.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://cfo.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/economy/indias-q2-fy27-gdp-growth-pegged-at-7-8-gva-growth-seen-at-8-8-2-sbi-research/135732270?utm_source=top_news&utm_medium=sectionListing">Economic Times</a> for the SBI Research forecast; <a href="https://bankofbaroda.bank.in/-/media/project/bob/countrywebsites/india/economic-scenario/data-releases/2026/bob-economics-q2fy27-gdp-preview-16-15.pdf">Bank of Baroda</a> for the 7.4-7.5% forecast and the monsoon impact; <a href="https://www.moneycontrol.com/news/business/economy/india-s-gdp-growth-pegged-at-7-3-in-fy26-27-may-touch-7-7-next-quarter-eac-pm-chairman-14082764.html">Moneycontrol</a> for the EAC-PM chairman's full year view and export target.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>What's the India GDP growth forecast for Q2 FY27?</h3>
<p>SBI Research forecasts about 7.8%, while Bank of Baroda expects 7.4% to 7.5%. The official figure from MOSPI is due later in November.</p>
<h3>How fast is India's economy growing this year?</h3>
<p>The EAC-PM chairman expects 7.3% growth in FY27, with about 7.7% next quarter, and the country is targeting $1 trillion in exports.</p>
