-- nepal-himalayan-climate-mechanism-20261125 (news; Nepal; verifiedAt NULL)
INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt", "verifiedAt", "authorName")
VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'nepal-himalayan-climate-mechanism-20261125', 'news', 'Nepal',
'Nepal Himalayan climate plan still not ready, India waits',
'Nepal Himalayan climate plan still not ready, India waits',
'Nepal Himalayan climate plan still not ready, two months after PM Balen Shah proposed the mechanism at the United Nations.',
'Nepal Himalayan climate plan still not ready, two months after PM Balen Shah proposed the mechanism at the United Nations.',
$$<p>The Nepal Himalayan Climate Plan is still not ready. Two months after PM Balendra Shah put forward at the UN a trilateral Himalayan Climate Resilience Mechanism with India and China at the United Nations.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Event</td><td>Nepal Himalayan climate mechanism stalled</td></tr>
<tr><td>Proposal</td><td>24 September 2026, 81st UN General Assembly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Proposed by</td><td>Prime Minister Balendra (Balen) Shah</td></tr>
<tr><td>Status</td><td>No concrete proposal shared with India or China yet</td></tr>
<tr><td>India position</td><td>Awaiting a formal proposal, says Foreign Secretary Misri</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>Two months after Shah floated the Nepal/India/China mechanism at the UN General Assembly, Nepal has still not produced a tangible proposal to present to either of its two neighbours (<a href="https://myrepublica.nagariknetwork.com/news/two-months-after-balens-un-proposal-nepal-yet-to-present-himalayan-climate--70-22.html">MyRepublica</a>, 25th November). The proposal came in the wake of growing climate-induced disasters in the Himalayas, most notably the Bhotekoshi floods which had a cross border dimension. A further indication of India's interest came in the form of the joint statement by Shah and PM Modi. At a press conference on Monday, Indian Foreign Secretary Vikram Misri said India was awaiting a formal proposal from Nepal.</p>

<p>Officials say work has in fact already begun (though building a framework across several ministries and two neighbouring countries will require a great deal of consultation). Lok Bahadur Chetri, Spokesperson for the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, confirmed that work on the mechanism had started and that the various ministries were being coordinated. Dr Maheshwor Dhakal, Spokesperson for the Ministry of Agriculture, Forest and Environment, said the Foreign Ministry had asked his ministry to put together an action plan and was in the process of doing so, utilising ICIMOD's knowledge and resources. The climate proposal was one of the outcomes of Shah's Delhi visit which also resulted in a Rs 240 billion Indian development package and eight MoUs. <a href="https://thehimalayantimes.com/nepal/pms-delhi-visit-puts-ties-back-on-a-positive-path">The Himalayan Times</a> has reported on this.</p>

<h2>What the Nepal Himalayan climate delay means</h2>

<p>The delay is significant as the Himalayas aren't going to wait: floods, glacial melt and landslides all cross borders and a mechanism which exists purely on paper cannot be the answer to joint funding, early warning or relief. For Nepal it was also a diplomatic test in itself: a small country asking its two massive neighbours to work together on its own initiative. The fact that India is publicly waiting for a document means the initiative is back in Kathmandu's hands. The jobs element is very real too: climate resilience work is going to require engineers, hydrologists, disaster managers and community workers. The Rs 240bn package that was agreed in Delhi includes grant funding for post-disaster reconstruction. You can follow the Delhi visit in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/balen-shah-modi-talks-outcome-nepal-india-20261124">Balen Shah-Modi talks outcome</a> piece and our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/nepal">Nepal hub</a> will be tracking the Nepali jobs and development story.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>The sources make no comment as to when the proposal will be ready, what it will contain (no draft, no date, no timetable is provided). They give no indication at all as to whether China has responded to it (only India's position is reported). There is no mention of the amount of funding that the mechanism would require and no comment on whether the Rs 240 billion Delhi package is in any way linked to it.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://myrepublica.nagariknetwork.com/news/two-months-after-balens-un-proposal-nepal-yet-to-present-himalayan-climate--70-22.html">MyRepublica</a> for the stalled proposal and the officials' comments; <a href="https://thehimalayantimes.com/nepal/pms-delhi-visit-puts-ties-back-on-a-positive-path">The Himalayan Times</a> for the outcomes of the Delhi visit and the development package.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>What is the Himalayan Climate Resilience Mechanism?</h3>
<p>A trilateral mechanism proposed by Nepal's prime minister at the UN on 24 September 2026, involving Nepal, India and China, to cooperate on climate disasters in the Himalayas.</p>
<h3>Why has Nepal not presented the plan yet?</h3>
<p>Officials say work has begun, but a framework spanning several ministries and two neighbouring countries needs extensive consultation, and no concrete proposal has been finalised.</p>
