$$<p>Nvidia shares were up over 30% on Monday 23 November 2026 following the reinstatement of Jensen Huang as CEO. The gains were reversed on Tuesday as the market cooled and with President Trump suggesting the possibility of a US government stake in the chipmaker.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Event</td><td>Nvidia shares after Jensen Huang reinstatement</td></tr>
<tr><td>Monday 23 Nov</td><td>Shares up 31.48%, best day since 2003</td></tr>
<tr><td>Market value gained</td><td>$952 billion in a single day</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tuesday 24 Nov</td><td>Shares fell 5.9% as the rally faded</td></tr>
<tr><td>Next milestone</td><td>Huang testifies before Congress in early December</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>The board unanimously voted to restore Huang as President, CEO and Director (reversing their position from just a week previously when they had ruled out his return), and the announcement immediately sent the market into overdrive. According to <a href="https://www.cnn.com/2026/11/23/markets/us-stocks-rebound-intl?cid=external-feeds_iluminar_meta">CNN</a>, Nvidia shares were up as much as 36.6% during the day and finished the day up 31.48% (a single day gain of $952bn in market value), the Nasdaq Composite was up 6.51% and the S&P&P 500 was up 4.73%. The return came after Huang had resigned on 12th November following the revelations of his extramarital affairs (one of which was with Alice Huang, a Chinese businesswoman who had been in contact with a Chinese intelligence officer).</p>

<p>Tuesday brought the comedown. <a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/amp/economy/2026/11/24/nvidia-stock-cools-after-huangs-return-driven-surge">Al Jazeera</a> reported that Nvidia stock reversed course as trading began, falling more than 5%, and <a href="https://www.investors.com/market-trend/stock-market-today/dow-jones-futures-nvidia-anthropic-ai-retreat-fed-inflation-data/">Investor's Business Daily</a> put the Tuesday fall at 5.9%. On Tuesday, Huang told staff at an hour-long meeting at Nvidia's Santa Clara headquarters that he had violated some of the company's code of conduct, <a href="https://www.channelnewsasia.com/world/nvidia-jensen-huang-violated-code-conduct-6578181">Channel News Asia</a> reported, citing a person present, while also saying it was untrue that he had been reinstated because of pressure from Trump. In an interview with the New York Post, Trump said he had thought about a US government equity stake in Nvidia, similar to the near-10% stake taken in Intel, adding "it's probably not too late", and said he would pardon Huang if necessary but expected it would not be needed.</p>

<h2>What the Nvidia shares swing means for AI jobs</h2>

<p>The two-day swing captures the whole AI jobs story in miniature: on Monday the market treated Huang's return as the removal of the biggest uncertainty hanging over the AI build-out, and on Tuesday it remembered that the investigations have not gone away. Huang is due to testify before Congress in early December, and the board's own investigations into foreign intelligence efforts, national security, and export controls are still underway. For workers, the practical read is that Nvidia is stable again at the top but still under political scrutiny, so hiring is likely to stay cautious until the testimony is behind it. The episode also shows how exposed the AI sector is to one company: a single board decision moved the entire Nasdaq by more than 6%. Growentix covered the saga from the start, including the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/jensen-huang-rehire-trump-nvidia-20261124">Trump rehire call</a>, the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/nvidia-sec-accounting-probe-huang-senate-testimony-20261122">SEC probe, and Senate testimony</a>, and our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/countries">country's hub</a> tracks how US chip policy affects hiring market by market.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>No source says the board has agreed to any government stake: Trump stressed he had not yet raised the idea with the company, and no proposal exists. The sources do not say what exactly Huang admitted to staff beyond the code-of-conduct remark, or which provisions were involved. One figure differs slightly: CNN put Monday's market-value gain at $952 billion, while other outlets round it to nearly $1 trillion. The sources also do not establish that Alice Huang spied for China; US officials have said no such conclusion has been reached.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.cnn.com/2026/11/23/markets/us-stocks-rebound-intl?cid=external-feeds_iluminar_meta">CNN</a> for the Monday share surge and market reaction; <a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/amp/economy/2026/11/24/nvidia-stock-cools-after-huangs-return-driven-surge">Al Jazeera</a> for the Tuesday reversal; <a href="https://www.channelnewsasia.com/world/nvidia-jensen-huang-violated-code-conduct-6578181">Channel News Asia</a> for the Huang staff meeting and Trump's stake and pardon comments; <a href="https://www.investors.com/market-trend/stock-market-today/dow-jones-futures-nvidia-anthropic-ai-retreat-fed-inflation-data/">Investor's Business Daily</a> for the Tuesday fall and the futures.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>How much did Nvidia shares jump on Huang's return?</h3>
<p>They closed up 31.48% on Monday 23rd November, the best day since 2003, adding $952bn in market value.</p>
<h3>Is the US government taking a stake in Nvidia?</h3>
<p>No proposal has been put forward. Trump told the NY Post he'd thought about it but hadn't actually raised it and said it was "probably not too late".</p>
