-- thanksgiving-travel-2026-aaa-20261125 (news; USA; verifiedAt NULL)
INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt", "verifiedAt", "authorName")
VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'thanksgiving-travel-2026-aaa-20261125', 'news', 'USA',
'Thanksgiving travel nears record as fares jump 17%',
'Thanksgiving travel nears record as fares jump 17%',
'Thanksgiving travel will see 81.5 million Americans on the move, AAA forecasts, with flights averaging $800 as the record streak ends on cost.',
'Thanksgiving travel will see 81.5 million Americans on the move, AAA forecasts, with flights averaging $800 as the record streak ends on cost.',
$$<p>Thanksgiving travel will see 81.5 million Americans on the move this year according to AAA's forecast. It's a holiday that's more about how much it costs you to get there.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Event</td><td>Thanksgiving travel, 24-30 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>Total travellers</td><td>81.5 million, down 0.1% on 2025</td></tr>
<tr><td>By car</td><td>72.9 million</td></tr>
<tr><td>By air</td><td>5.96 million domestic flyers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Average round-trip fare</td><td>$800, up 17% on last year</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>AAA has 81.5m Americans projected to travel at least 50 miles from home between Tuesday 24th and Monday 30th November (<a href="https://terminaltracker.co/blog/aaa-thanksgiving-2026-travel-forecast-flights-fares-top-destinations">Terminal Tracker</a> report), a tiny dip on 16 November. That is a hair below last year's 81.6m and the end of three years of record Thanksgivings. It's not that people have stopped travelling, but because getting there got expensive. About 72.9 million people will drive, 200,000 fewer than in 2025) largely due to record pump prices, with domestic flyers at 5.96m (a 0.2% fall). Buses, trains and cruises grow, at 2.6m (up 4.1%).</p>

<p>The price tag is the story. AAA's booking data puts the average domestic round-trip flight at about $800, up 17% from last year. Airlines point to fuel: Airlines for America says the jet fuel spot price averaged a record $4.64 a gallon in October, and the US Energy Information Administration's Gulf Coast benchmark averaged $4.483, more than double the $2.154 of a year earlier. The Federal Aviation Administration projects roughly 417,000 commercial flights between 23 November and 1 December, the busiest holiday skies in 16 years, <a href="https://www.thetraveler.org/us-thanksgiving-2026-travel-faces-near-record-crowds-and-storms/">The Traveller</a> reported. The biggest question is whether the Transportation Security Administration will set a new screening record on Sunday 29 November: the record stands at 3,134,613 people screened on Sunday 30 November 2025, and the TSA has said early indicators suggest more than 3 million that day. Coastal storms and the memory of a nationwide aviation technology outage less than two weeks ago add to the risk of disruption.</p>

<h2>What Thanksgiving travel means for jobs</h2>

<p>Thanksgiving week is the airline, airport and hotel industry's biggest stress test. You can see the hiring reflected in the numbers: AA is expecting over 8m customers on nearly 85,000 flights between 19 November and 1 December, United expects more than 500,000 customers a day and Amtrak is adding capacity on the NEC. For the seasonal workers it's peak earning time but the cost squeeze works the other way with carriers cutting out the less profitable routes, retiring the older aircraft and raising the checked bag fees to make up for fuel. The most you can be the most by avoiding Sunday 29 November, the expected busiest day. Growentix covered the aviation disruption earlier in the week in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/delta-flight-delays-thanksgiving-outage-20261124">Delta flight delays</a> story and our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/usa">USA jobs</a> hub has the seasonal hiring in travel and hospitality.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>The sources don't actually give us a figure for how many people travelled as the AAA figure is a forecast published on 16th November and not a count at all. The TSA record is merely a possibility for Sunday. The $800 figure is based on what the travellers actually paid when they booked rather than some market wide fare index so it's a market-wide fare index, so it measures what families actually spent rather than the average fare on sale. The sources don't actually say which of the coastal airports will be the worst hit by the storms either, just that flights, driving and ferry links could be affected.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://terminaltracker.co/blog/aaa-thanksgiving-2026-travel-forecast-flights-fares-top-destinations">Terminal Tracker</a> for the AAA forecast figures and top destinations; <a href="https://travelmarketnews.com/thanksgiving-2026-travel-forecast-aaa-airlines-tsa-november-2026">Travel Market News</a> for the AAA and Airlines for America comparison and TSA analysis; <a href="https://www.thetraveler.org/us-thanksgiving-2026-travel-faces-near-record-crowds-and-storms/">The Traveller</a> for the FAA flight forecast and storm risks.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>How many people are going for Thanksgiving 2026?</h3>
<p>AAA forecasts 81.5m Americans travelling at least 50 miles from home between the 24th and 30th of November, just below last year's 81.6m.</p>
<h3>What's the busiest Thanksgiving travel day?</h3>
<p>Sunday 29th November is going to be the busiest day to fly with the TSA likely to screen over 3 million passengers which is nearly the record of 3,134,613.</p>
