-- trump-tariffs-china-eu-20261125 (news; USA; verifiedAt NULL)
INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt", "verifiedAt", "authorName")
VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'trump-tariffs-china-eu-20261125', 'news', 'USA',
'Trump tariffs plan targets China and EU in new trade move',
'Trump tariffs plan targets China and EU in new trade move',
'Trump tariffs are set to rise on China, the EU and 14 other economies as the US prepares new duties to replace levies struck down by the Supreme Court.',
'Trump tariffs are set to rise on China, the EU and 14 other economies as the US prepares new duties to replace levies struck down by the Supreme Court.',
$$<p>Trump tariffs are due to go up on China, the EU and 14 other countries as the administration prepares new import tariffs to take the place of the wider tariffs which the Supreme Court invalidated earlier this year.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Event</td><td>New US tariffs planned after subsidy investigation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Date reported</td><td>24 November 2026, announcement expected imminently</td></tr>
<tr><td>Economies covered</td><td>China, EU and 14 others, 16 in total</td></tr>
<tr><td>Legal basis</td><td>Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974</td></tr>
<tr><td>Earlier duties</td><td>April 2025 levies ruled unconstitutional in February 2026</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>The US Trade Representative's office is due to release the findings of a months-long investigation into industrial subsidies from the 16 economies in the coming days, two sources familiar with the matter told the <a href="https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/world/20261125/us-ready-to-slap-tariffs-on-china-eu-and-others-over-subsidized-production-ap-sources-say">Korea Times</a> (via AP). <a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/trump-administration-set-to-unveil-new-tariffs-after-probeon-excess-factory-capacity-sources/article71740905.ece">The Hindu</a>, carrying Reuters, reported the findings were expected as early as Tuesday afternoon, with a broad range of goods. Bloomberg and the WSJ were the first to report on the plans. The White House and the USTR have not responded to requests for comment and the sources noted the timing could still be different.</p>

<p>The duties would be aimed at what the White House describes as excessive manufacturing output (fuelled by subsidies) which results in an oversupply of cheaper goods into the US and is thereby detrimental to US industry. The countries of concern are China, India, Japan, South Korea, Mexico, Vietnam, Taiwan, Thailand, Malaysia, Indonesia, Switzerland, Norway, Singapore, Bangladesh and Cambodia, plus the EU. The new duties would in part replace the April 2025 duties which the Supreme Court struck down as unconstitutional in February. In July the administration imposed duties of 10-12.5% on goods from some 60 countries for alleged failure to enforce bans on goods made with forced labour; those duties are being challenged at the Court of International Trade. The new duties are expected to remain within the caps set out in the various trade agreements (15% for the EU, Japan and South Korea; 19% for Malaysia; 20% for China) as long as the trade truce which is due to expire on 10th January is extended.</p>

<h2>What the Trump tariffs plan means for jobs</h2>

<p>For workers the tariffs cut both ways and you can see both sides of the issue in the same week. They can protect US manufacturing jobs in the short term. They can also lead to increased costs for US companies that import parts and the administration's own tariffs have in part been responsible for the higher prices we've seen on consumer goods. A Reuters/Ipsos poll following the November midterms found 62% of people would back an effort to repeal the tariffs and the midterms were in large part driven by the issue of affordability. The market reaction was instant with the news of the new tariffs weighing on the markets on Tuesday and helping to push Bitcoin below $60,000. For job seekers in the export industries in India, Vietnam, Bangladesh and the other countries named the tariffs could mean slower orders and therefore quieter hiring if they come in at the upper end of the expected range. Growentix monitors the trade picture through our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-stock-selloff-sp-500-correction-20261124">market coverage</a> and our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/countries">country hub</a> which tracks the impact of US trade policy on the hiring market on a market by market basis.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>No source says the tariffs have actually been announced: every report describes plans and expected findings, not a published decision, and the timing could still change. The rates are also unsettled, and the estimates disagree sharply: Deborah Elms of the Hinrich Foundation said the newest tariffs could add 2% to 7.5% if the aim is to restore pre-court levels. In contrast, Ryan Majerus of King and Spalding said the administration could propose 15% to 25% on imports from countries deemed to have excess capacity. The sources do not say which products will be covered, when the duties would take effect, or whether Mexico and Norway will face immediate levies.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/world/20261125/us-ready-to-slap-tariffs-on-china-eu-and-others-over-subsidized-production-ap-sources-say">Korea Times</a> (for the AP report on the planned announcement); <a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/trump-administration-set-to-unveil-new-tariffs-after-probeon-excess-factory-capacity-sources/article71740905.ece">The Hindu</a> (for the Reuters report on the probe and the tariff caps); <a href="https://www.moneycontrol.com/world/us-prepares-fresh-tariff-hikes-on-china-eu-and-other-trading-partners-article-14088242.html">Moneycontrol</a> (for the Section 301 background and rate estimates).</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Have the new Trump tariffs been announced?</h3>
<p>Not yet. Sources indicate the USTR findings and the new duties are due imminently (possibly as early as this week) but no announcement has yet been made at the time of writing.</p>
<h3>Which countries are the subject of the tariff probe?</h3>
<p>China, India, Japan, South Korea, Mexico, Vietnam, Taiwan, Thailand, Malaysia, Indonesia, Switzerland, Norway, Singapore, Bangladesh and Cambodia and the EU.</p>
$$,
