-- ukraine-peace-talks-abu-dhabi-20261125 (news; Ukraine; verifiedAt NULL)
INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt", "verifiedAt", "authorName")
VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'ukraine-peace-talks-abu-dhabi-20261125', 'news', 'Ukraine',
'Ukraine peace talks end without deal as January deadline looms',
'Ukraine peace talks end without deal as January deadline looms',
'Ukraine peace talks in Abu Dhabi ended without a breakthrough, as Zelenskyy said Trump''s envoys want a deal by January and Kyiv seeks more US aid.',
'Ukraine peace talks in Abu Dhabi ended without a breakthrough, as Zelenskyy said Trump''s envoys want a deal by January and Kyiv seeks more US aid.',
$$<p>Ukraine peace talks in Abu Dhabi have come to an end with no breakthrough. The US has put forward proposals for security guarantees and there have been claims that the gap between the two sides had in fact narrowed.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Event</td><td>US-mediated Ukraine-Russia talks, Abu Dhabi</td></tr>
<tr><td>Outcome</td><td>No major progress or breakthrough</td></tr>
<tr><td>First talks since</td><td>February 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>US envoys</td><td>Jared Kushner and Steve Witkoff</td></tr>
<tr><td>Target date</td><td>Peace deal ideally before January 2027</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>Negotiators from Russia and Ukraine have wrapped up their US-mediated talks in Abu Dhabi with no real breakthrough, according to <a href="https://nexuseuropa.eu/en/story/us-brokered-talks-between-ukraine-and-russia-yield-no-major-progress-28074">Nexus Europa</a>. This is the first such round of bilateral talks since February. The meetings came after comments from US President Donald Trump on potential terms for ending the war. Former US diplomat Kurt Volker has been critical of the Trump approach, stating that the US team should not be allowing Moscow to steer the talks towards wider European security reform and should instead be applying direct pressure on the Kremlin.</p>

<p> Zelenskyy has said that Kushner and Witkoff want a deal to be struck as quickly as possible, ideally before January, according to <a href="https://www.pravda.com.ua/eng/news/2026/11/24/8064391/">Ukrainska Pravda</a> who are quoting from his Bloomberg interview. Ukraine would be looking for a significant package of military aid from the US and for much tougher sanctions on Russia if no deal acceptable to Kyiv is reached. <a href="https://www.military.com/zelenskyy-wants-congress-to-pressure-putin-if-trump-cant-win-deal">Military.com</a> reports that Zelenskyy would also be in contact with the new Democrat controlled US Congress to put further pressure on Russia if Trump is unable to get Putin to end the war by the end of the year. Kushner and Witkoff have put forward the idea of Ukraine giving up the Luhansk and Donetsk regions (the Donbas) in return for some form of NATO security assurances. A meeting with the Coalition of the Willing is being planned in Berlin but Russia has said it has not agreed to European participation in the next round.</p>

<h2>What the Ukraine peace talks mean for jobs</h2>

<p>A war that drags on keeps Europe's biggest jobs story on ice: Ukrainian reconstruction, European defence hiring and the energy markets all turn on how this plays out. A deal in January would see reconstruction (the construction, the engineering, the logistics) become the biggest employment programme in Europe. No deal would see defence spending continue to rise and energy prices remain volatile, which would have a knock-on effect for every other employer. Zelenskyy's warning about the war is still going on in the air every single night. For the negotiators say. For workers watching from Nepal and South Asia, the direct impact is on energy and food prices which are in turn dictated by the headlines from these negotiations. We've been tracking the economic fallout from the war in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ukraine-prisoner-exchange-abu-dhabi-talks-20261120">coverage of the Abu Dhabi talks </a> and the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/countries">country's hub</a>.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>The sources give us absolutely no indication as to when the next round of talks will take place (no date has been agreed and Russia has not accepted the participation of the EU). They tell us nothing at all about what the security guarantees would actually look like, or whether Ukraine would be prepared to give in on Donbas (Zelenskyy made it very clear that concessions at Ukraine's expense were the very thing to be feared). They don't say how much military assistance Kyiv would be seeking, only that it would be significant.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://nexuseuropa.eu/en/story/us-brokered-talks-between-ukraine-and-russia-yield-no-major-progress-28074">Nexus Europa</a> for the Abu Dhabi outcome and Volker's criticism; <a href="https://www.pravda.com.ua/eng/news/2026/11/24/8064391/">Ukrainska Pravda</a> for Zelenskyy's Bloomberg interview and the January target; <a href="https://www.military.com/zelenskyy-wants-congress-to-pressure-putin-if-trump-cant-win-deal">Military.com</a> for the Congress plan and the Donbas proposals.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Did the Abu Dhabi Ukraine peace talks result in a deal?</h3>
<p>No. The US-mediated talks between Russia and Ukraine have come to nothing. There was no breakthrough at all, despite the proposals for security guarantees.</p>
<h3>When do Trump's envoys want a Ukraine peace deal?</h3>
<p>As soon as possible, ideally before January 2027, according to President Zelenskyy.</p>
$$,
