$$<p>Mariah Carey All I Want for Christmas Is You has rocketed from No. 18 to No. 4 on the Billboard Hot 100, its first top-five showing on a November chart. The 1994 festive classic leads the Christmas surge on the Hot 100 dated 28 November 2026, with Wham!'s "Last Christmas" and Bobby Helms' "Jingle Bell Rock" also in the top 10.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Song</td><td>"All I Want for Christmas Is You" — Mariah Carey</td></tr>
<tr><td>Released</td><td>1994 (co-written with Walter Afanasieff)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Billboard Hot 100</td><td>No. 4 (chart dated 28 November 2026), up from No. 18</td></tr>
<tr><td>Latest week</td><td>13.5 million US streams; radio impressions up 108%</td></tr>
<tr><td>UK Singles Chart</td><td>No. 18 (chart week 20–26 November 2026), up 18 places</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hot 100 record</td><td>22 cumulative weeks at No. 1, the previous all-time record</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>The Christmas take-over came a week earlier than normal this year. According to Billboard's chart report (as cited by <a href="https://the-vault-tv.com/2026/11/24/four-of-the-hot-100s-top-five-belong-to-sony-christmas-helped/">The Vault</a>), Carey's song climbed 14 places on 13.5m streams, with radio airplay impressions up 108% in the week. Wham!'s "Last Christmas" went from 14 to 5 on 13.8m streams (radio up 127%) and "Jingle Bell Rock" made a jump from 28 to 9. That's three Christmas songs in the Hot 100 top 10. Ella Langley's "Choosin' Texas" is still at No.1 and the gap is closing (Langley's streaming lead over the carol is down to around 3.4m in the week that one number went down and the other up by a third). In the UK the song has also risen 18 places to 18 on the Official Singles Chart (<a href="https://www.officialcharts.com/chart-news/sombr-jamie-miller-justice-gta-6-number-1-singles-chart/">Official Charts Company</a>). Our report on the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/wham-last-christmas-mariah-carey-hot-100-20261121">Wham! and Mariah Carey Hot 100 returns</a> covered the initial stages of the seasonal climb and the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/popmusic">Pop Music</a> hub has the rest of the week's chart news.</p>

<h2>What the Mariah Carey All I Want for Christmas Is You surge means</h2>

<p>This is the first time the song has ever reached the Hot 100 top five in a November chart (and therefore the question at the top of the No. 1 was how long Langley's record run would last; now there is a genuine challenger, and the next chart is the first one to have a full week of holiday listening in its favour. The song's history makes the threat very real; it's had 22 weeks at No. 1 in total (Langley only passed that record in September) and it's been to the top almost every December. The UK picture is very different — despite topping the Official Singles Chart on three occasions, the song has never managed the Christmas No. 1. That little quirk keeps the UK race open.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>Nothing in the piece states whether Carey will overtake Langley next week, or whether she has any new Christmas release or performance scheduled for the season. The sources do not give a sales figure for the current chart week so we don't know the precise mix of streams, radio and sales that has led to the No. 4 jump is not stated. They give no indication of how the song is performing on streaming in the UK, just its chart position.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://the-vault-tv.com/2026/11/24/four-of-the-hot-100s-top-five-belong-to-sony-christmas-helped/">The Vault</a>, <a href="https://ratingsgamemusic.com/2026/11/23/top-5-singles-on-the-billboard-hot-100-this-week-11-23-ella-langley-stella-lefty-tame-impala/">RGM</a>, <a href="https://www.officialcharts.com/chart-news/sombr-jamie-miller-justice-gta-6-number-1-singles-chart/">Official Charts Company</a>.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>What position is 'All I Want for Christmas Is You' on the Hot 100?</h3>
<p>No. 4 on the chart as published on 28 November 2026 (up from No. 18 the previous week) which is its first top-five showing on a November chart.</p>
<h3>How many weeks in total has the song been at Number 1?</h3>
<p>22 cumulative weeks at No. 1 on the Hot 100 record, passed by Ella Langley's "Choosin' Texas" in September 2026.</p>
