-- dell-ai-server-sales-20261125 (tech-ai; USA; verifiedAt NULL)
INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt", "verifiedAt", "authorName")
VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'dell-ai-server-sales-20261125', 'tech-ai', 'USA',
'Dell AI server sales triple as revenue doubles',
'Dell AI server sales triple as revenue doubles',
'Dell AI server sales tripled as AI server revenue jumped 291% to $22 billion, lifting quarterly revenue 103% to $54.9 billion.',
'Dell AI server sales tripled as AI server revenue jumped 291% to $22 billion, lifting quarterly revenue 103% to $54.9 billion.',
$$<p>Dell AI server sales tripled in the third quarter, with AI server revenue was up 291% to $22bn and total quarterly revenue more than doubled to $54.9bn, beating estimates. This is the third time this year.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Company</td><td>Dell Technologies</td></tr>
<tr><td>Quarter</td><td>Fiscal 2027 third quarter, ended 30 October 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>Revenue</td><td>$54.9 billion, up 103% year on year</td></tr>
<tr><td>Adjusted earnings per share</td><td>$9.45, against $6.59 expected</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI server revenue</td><td>$22.0 billion, up 291% year on year</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI server backlog</td><td>$132.3 billion, a record</td></tr>
<tr><td>Full-year revenue guidance</td><td>$202 billion, raised from $192 billion</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>Dell reported record revenue of $54.9 bn for the three months to 30 October 2026 (up 103% Yoy) and well above the $49.32 bn that analysts had expected, according to <a href="https://www.reuters.com/business/dell-lifts-annual-forecasts-after-record-quarter-surging-ai-server-demand-2026-11-24/">Reuters</a>. Adjusted EPS was $9.45 vs $6.59 expected, net profit was $5.67 bn. The results were issued post market close on Tuesday 24 November.</p>

<p>The growth was almost entirely from Infrastructure Solutions Group (servers, networking and storage) which saw revenue nearly triple to $42.0bn (up 198%). AI-optimised server revenue was up 291% to $22.0bn, traditional server and networking revenue up 235% to $15.0bn and storage revenue up 24% to $4.9bn. Dell booked $60.2bn of new AI server orders in the quarter and finished with a record $132.3bn of AI backlog.</p>

<p>The PC business grew a lot more slowly. Client Solutions Group revenue was up 3% to $12.89bn, down from 20% in the prior quarter as the AI buildout rather than PC was the driver of the quarter. COO Jeff Clarke said Dell was a very different company now with the infrastructure group being the driver of both profit and growth and he did highlight that each of the $60.2bn in new orders had been properly assessed on customer quality and on access to capital, land and power.</p>

<p>Dell also raised its guidance across the board. For fiscal 2027, revenue is now expected to be $202bn (up 78% year on year) versus the prior guidance of $192bn and Wall Street's expectation of around $194.5bn. The full year AI server revenue guidance is now $78bn (up from $74bn) and the adjusted EPS guidance is now $29.68 (up from $25.50). For the fourth quarter, revenue is now expected to be around $56.3bn and adjusted EPS is $8.40.</p>

<h2>What the Dell AI server sales mean</h2>

<p>The numbers are a perfect illustration of how completely Dell has been transformed by the AI infrastructure boom. A year ago you'd still have described it as a PC company. This quarter the server and storage business is generating three quarters of total revenue and the AI servers alone are generating more than the entire PC business. The $132.3bn backlog (which is about two thirds of the full year revenue that Dell is now expecting) gives the revised forecast a real basis rather than just a hope.</p>

<p>The Clarke vetting comment is also important. With $60.2bn of orders booked in a single quarter, the question has been on everyone's lips as to whether every single buyer can actually afford and power the hardware. Dell says it looks at customer capital, land and power before it counts an order, directly addressing the financing concerns that have been hanging over the AI buildout. The contrast with the PC business which saw growth stall at 3% really does highlight the fact that the growth is in the data centre hardware, not the desktops. Our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/dell-earnings-november-24-preview-ai-backlog-20261123">preview prior to the results</a> set out what the analysts were looking for, with more on the wider field in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/tech-ai">Tech news</a> section.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>Dell's release provides absolutely no breakdown of which customers the $60.2bn of new AI orders came from, and gives no AI server profit margin at all so we can't work out how much of the $22bn in AI revenue actually translated into profit. There's no indication in the release of how much of the $132.3bn of backlog will turn into revenue in the current quarter or what share depends on customers getting the power and the financing. Nothing at all is said about memory supply constraints despite the fact that memory is now a significant cost to other hardware suppliers. The initial share price reaction was mixed so no clear market view is offered here.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.dell.com/en-us/newsroom/announcements/dell-technologies-delivers-third-quarter-fiscal-2027-financial-results">Dell's official results release</a> for all the headline figures, segment revenue, orders, backlog and guidance; <a href="https://www.reuters.com/business/dell-lifts-annual-forecasts-after-record-quarter-surging-ai-server-demand-2026-11-24/">Reuters</a> for the analyst estimates and the forecast comparison; <a href="https://www.marketbeat.com/instant-alerts/transcript-dell-technologies-q3-earnings-call-highlights-2026-11-24/">MarketBeat</a> for the earnings call highlights and fourth-quarter outlook.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>How much did Dell increase its full year revenue guidance by?</h3>
<p>Dell has raised its full year 2027 revenue guidance by $10bn to $202bn from $192bn, the third increase in as many years. The AI server revenue guidance is up to $78bn from $74bn.</p>

<h3>What's in Dell's AI server backlog?</h3>
<p>Dell ended the quarter with a record $132.3bn AI server backlog following $60.2bn in new AI orders booked. Each of those orders has been assessed on the customer's quality and on their access to capital, land and power.</p>
