(gen_random_uuid(), 'hp-q4-earnings-ai-pcs-dividend-20261125', 'tech-ai', 'USA',
'HP Q4 earnings beat estimates as AI PCs hit 60%',
'HP Q4 earnings beat estimates as AI PCs hit 60%',
'HP Q4 earnings beat forecasts with $16 billion in quarterly revenue as AI PCs reached nearly 60% of shipments, and the dividend rose to $0.31.',
'HP Q4 earnings beat forecasts with $16 billion in quarterly revenue as AI PCs reached nearly 60% of shipments, and the dividend rose to $0.31.',
$$<p>HP Q4 earnings beat the forecasts on 24 November 2026 with fourth-quarter revenue of $16bn and AI PCs at nearly 60% of the shipments. The company also raised its quarterly dividend to $0.31 per share.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Company</td><td>HP Inc.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Quarter</td><td>Fiscal 2026 fourth quarter, ended October 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>Revenue</td><td>$16.0 billion, up 9.3% year on year</td></tr>
<tr><td>Adjusted earnings per share</td><td>$0.83, against $0.75 expected</td></tr>
<tr><td>Full-year revenue</td><td>$60.5 billion, up 9.5% year on year</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI PCs</td><td>Nearly 60% of shipments in the quarter</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dividend</td><td>$0.31 per share, payable 6 January 2027</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>HP reported fourth-quarter net revenue of $16.0bn, up 9.3% year on year and beating the $15.42bn that analysts had expected, according to <a href="https://www.wsj.com/business/earnings/hp-offers-prudent-guidance-amid-rising-memory-costs-37c67b9d">the WSJ</a>. Adjusted EPS was $0.83, down from $0.93 a year ago but above the $0.75 that analysts had forecast. Net profit was $769m or $0.84 a share on a GAAP basis, flat on the year-ago figure. The results were released after the US market closed on Tuesday 24 November.</p>

<p>Personal Systems (the PC business) was the story of the quarter. Revenue up 14% Yoy to $11.8 bn, operating margin a mere 3.0%. Printing revenue down 1% to $4.3 bn, operating margin a very healthy 19.7%. Full year revenue was $60.5 bn (up 9.5%), non-GAAP EPS was $3.33 (up 6.7% Yoy) and free cash flow was $3.3 bn. The company returned $2.3 bn to shareholders via buybacks and dividends during the year.</p>

<p>The standout detail came from interim chief executive Bruce Broussard, who said AI PCs reached nearly 60% of shipments in the quarter, as HP grew its higher-value businesses while addressing memory and storage cost pressures. HP also raised its quarterly dividend to $0.31 per share, payable on 6 January 2027 to shareholders of record on 9 December 2026.</p>

<p>The guidance was very conservative. For the first quarter of fiscal 2027 HP is guiding to adjusted EPS of $0.66 to $0.76 and full year EPS of $3.15 to $3.45 with free cash flow of $2.9bn to $3.1bn. CFO Karen Parkhill said the 2027 guidance was a very conservative view of the continuing cost and demand pressures.</p>

<h2>What the HP Q4 earnings mean</h2>

<p>The quarter is telling two stories. The first is that the PC refresh is very much real and is increasingly an AI PC refresh: nearly six in ten HP PCS shipped in the quarter were AI PCs which is a massive shift for a category that didn't really exist two years ago. The second story is that selling those PCS is becoming harder to make money on. 3.0% margin in Personal Systems against 19.7% in Printing is a clear indication of the extent to which the revenue growth is being consumed by the cost of components, particularly memory and storage.</p>

<p>That squeeze is why the guidance is so careful. They beat the quarter, they've increased the dividend and still have very cautious guidance for the year as they're not at all sure that memory prices will ease. The dividend rise signals confidence in the cash generation with $3.3bn of free cash flow for the year, but the $2.9bn to $3.1bn free cash flow guidance for fiscal 2027 is a step down. The leadership change adds another dimension to the watchfulness: Emma Walmsley takes over as CEO on 4th January 2027, succeeding Broussard, inheriting a company where the biggest business is growing rapidly and is earning very thinly. Our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/hp-earnings-november-24-preview-pc-guidance-20261123">preview before the results</a> set out what the analysts were looking for and more on the wider field is in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/tech-ai">Tech news</a> section.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>HP's release doesn't give us the absolute number of AI PCs shipped (only the % of the total), so we don't have any information on the size of the AI PC business in units. There's no information on the increase in memory and storage costs in the quarter or how much of that HP passed on in the form of price increases. HP has provided earnings guidance for 2027 but no revenue guidance so we don't have any sales guidance for the year. The release says nothing about which PC models were responsible for the AI PC share or about demand in any particular region.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.hp.com/us-en/newsroom/press-releases/2026/hp-inc-reports-fiscal-2026-full-year-and-fourth-quarter-results.html">HP's official results release</a> for all revenue, earnings, segment, cash flow and dividend figures and the outlook; <a href="https://www.wsj.com/business/earnings/hp-offers-prudent-guidance-amid-rising-memory-costs-37c67b9d">the Wall Street Journal</a> for the analyst estimates, the guidance framing and the incoming CEO; <a href="https://www.zacks.com/stock/news/3034826/hp-hpq-tops-q4-earnings-and-revenue-estimates">Zacks</a> for the earnings and revenue beat against consensus.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>Did HP beat earnings?</h3>
<p>Yes. HP reported adjusted earnings of $0.83 per share (vs $0.75 expected) and revenue of $16bn (vs $15.42bn expected). Adjusted earnings were down from $0.93 a year earlier.</p>

<h3>What proportion of HP shipments were AI PCs?</h3>
<p>AI PCs reached nearly 60% of HP's shipments in the fourth quarter, according to interim chief executive Bruce Broussard. HP did not state the absolute number of AI PCs shipped.</p>
