INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt", "verifiedAt", "authorName")
VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'xpeng-worldgpt-ai-day-iron-20261125', 'tech-ai', 'China',
'XPENG WorldGPT: One AI Model for Cars, Robots and Drones',
'XPENG WorldGPT: One AI Model for Cars, Robots and Drones',
'XPENG WorldGPT is one AI model that learns across cars, boats, drones and robots, ahead of the AI Day showcase of humanoid robot IRON today.',
'XPENG WorldGPT is one AI model that learns across cars, boats, drones and robots, ahead of the AI Day showcase of humanoid robot IRON today.',
$$<p>XPENG WorldGPT is a single AI model that learns across cars, boats, drones and robots. It was announced by XPENG on 24 November 2026. It's being unveiled a day before XPENG's AI Day in Guangzhou where the company's humanoid robot IRON is the star of the show today.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Company</td><td>XPENG</td></tr>
<tr><td>Model</td><td>WorldGPT, part of the Turing World Model family</td></tr>
<tr><td>Unveiled</td><td>24 November 2026, Guangzhou, China</td></tr>
<tr><td>Machine types</td><td>Six: cars, boats, drones, single-arm robots, dual-arm robots, humanoid robots</td></tr>
<tr><td>Benchmark first places</td><td>12 across public benchmarks, as of 12 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI Day event</td><td>25 November 2026, 14:00 UTC+8, Guangzhou</td></tr>
<tr><td>Robot on show</td><td>IRON humanoid, about 170 cm tall</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>Xpeng have just unveiled WorldGPT as a world action model which enables all sorts of machines to understand, predict and interact with the physical world. It's part of the company's Turing World Model family and is designed to bring together learning across six machine types: cars, boats, drones, single arm robots, dual arm robots and humanoid robots. The concept is that a car deciding on the gap to the vehicle in front of it and a robotic arm deciding on the distance to a cup are both solving the same problem, so you can learn the common physics once and apply it across all of the machine types.</p>

<p>WorldGPT operates as a cloud-based teacher model. It takes in camera video and instructions and then predicts future video, plans actions, understands the scene and reasons in language. That shared knowledge is then distilled down into smaller on-device student models for each machine type. XPENG says the model has achieved 12 first place results on public benchmarks across the areas of autonomous driving, robotics, visual understanding and video generation as of 12th November 2026. Full results are available on the project website.</p>

<p>The reveal is for today's event. XPENG is hosting its 2026 AI Day (theme ORIGIN) on 25th November in Guangzhou, the eighth edition of its annual AI Day). The big feature is IRON, the company's general purpose humanoid robot. It's 170cm tall with 76 DOF across the body and 21 in each hand. It's powered by three in-house Turing AI chips (2,250 TOPS) which run XPENG's physical AI foundation model on device so it can do its work without needing to be teleoperated. There's a teaser video of IRON where it picks up a book, pours a drink and docks itself for charging.</p>

<h2>What the XPENG WorldGPT launch means</h2>

<p>WorldGPT is XPENG's answer to the single most expensive problem in robotics: every new robot generally requires its own data, its own training and its own tuning. A single teacher model that learns the physics once and then distils that into six different robot bodies would be a massive cost saving if it works as well in the real world as it does in their benchmarks. The 12 first place claims are the evidence they put forward, again their own interpretation of the public benchmark tables, not an independent test, so they should be treated as a claim to watch rather than a definitive result.</p>

<p>The timing is absolutely on purpose. WorldGPT gives the AI Day crowd the brain and IRON gives it the body. IRON is a robot that's already out of the prototype phase and on the production line, after eight years and serial production is due to start 1st January 2027 with external customer launch in the second quarter of 2027. The robotics business raised over $900m in August 2026 at a valuation of over $6.3bn (XPENG called it the largest single private financing round in the history of China's embodied AI industry). That capital, plus the demo today, is all going to be judged on whether IRON can actually do useful work without a human at the controls. The IRON story connects to our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/tesla-ai5-chip-optimus-cars-stay-ai4-20261124">Tesla AI5 chip and our Optimus</a> coverage of the competition in the humanoid space. More on the wider field in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/tech-ai">Tech news</a>.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>Xpeng have not indicated when (or indeed if) WorldGPT will be available to external developers and there is no public access or open weights at present. The sources do not told how large the model is in parameters, what it cost to train, or how much data from each of the six machine types went into it. The benchmark tables are Xpeng's own tables and there is no independent verification of the 12 results that have come top in the various categories. With IRON we're not given a price, no battery life and no list of the tasks the robot is currently capable of doing reliably outside of the staged demos. Nothing at all about the outcome of today's AI Day is known at the time of writing and no demo result is claimed.</p>

<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.xpeng.com/pressroom/news/01a20bcf44b3a1e838d68a02a2110153">XPENG's official press release</a> for the WorldGPT launch, the six machine types and the benchmark claims; <a href="https://www.automationinsider.in/xpeng-to-showcase-humanoid-robotics-and-physical-ai-innovations-at-ai-day-2026/">Automation Insider</a> for the AI Day programme, the IRON specs and the funding details; <a href="https://www.singularity.kiwi/xpeng-origin-ai-day-2026/">Singularity</a> for the timing of the event and the preview context.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>When is Xpeng's AI Day 2026?</h3>
<p>Xpeng's 2026 AI Day is on 25th November in Guangzhou, starting at 14:00 UTC+8, under the theme ORIGIN. It's the eighth edition of the company's annual AI showcase.</p>

<h3>What machines does WorldGPT cover?</h3>
<p>WorldGPT learns across six machine types: cars, boats, drones, single arm robots, dual arm robots and humanoids. It has a shared cloud teacher model which learns the knowledge and distils it down into smaller on-device models for each of the machines.</p>
