-- zhipu-glm-5-4-loop-transformer-20261125 (tech-ai; China; verifiedAt NULL)
INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt", "verifiedAt", "authorName")
VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'zhipu-glm-5-4-loop-transformer-20261125', 'tech-ai', 'China',
'Zhipu GLM-5.4 Loops Its Layers to Rival 2 Trillion Models',
'Zhipu GLM-5.4 Loops Its Layers to Rival 2 Trillion Models',
'Zhipu GLM-5.4 is a 750-billion-parameter model that runs its 28 layers twice, claiming the intelligence of models two to three trillion strong.',
'Zhipu GLM-5.4 is a 750-billion-parameter model that runs its 28 layers twice, claiming the intelligence of models two to three trillion strong.',
$$<p>Zhipu GLM-5.4 is their new flagship model from Z.ai, released 23rd November 2026. It runs its layers twice in order to extract as much intelligence as possible from a relatively small number of parameters. The company claim that the 750B model is on par with models of two to three trillion parameters.</p>

<table>
<tr><td>Company</td><td>Z.ai, also known as Zhipu AI</td></tr>
<tr><td>Model</td><td>GLM-5.4, first Loop Transformer flagship</td></tr>
<tr><td>Released</td><td>23 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>Parameters</td><td>About 750 billion</td></tr>
<tr><td>Architecture</td><td>28 layers of the GLM-5 base run twice per forward pass</td></tr>
<tr><td>Context window</td><td>1 million tokens, with vision support</td></tr>
<tr><td>Available now</td><td>GLM Coding Plan subscribers, ZCode and Z.ai Chat</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>Z.ai have just released GLM-5.4 as their first model based on the Loop Transformer architecture. It takes the 28 layer GLM-5 base model and runs each layer twice during a forward pass, giving the network more computational depth without adding parameters. The model has about 750 billion parameters, and the company says its intelligence is on par with models with two to three trillion parameters. Vision is included so it can see screenshots and images and the context window is one million tokens with a max output of 128,000 tokens.</p>

<p>The release is squarely aimed at coding. Z.ai positions it as the beginning of generalised coding where code is the base for all tasks beyond software. The demos they've shown include a self-narrated music video, a six axis robotic arm modelled in FreeCAD, a complete game (with physics, maps and combat), and an interactive 3D bird watching scene from geometry and custom shaders. It's live now for GLM Coding Plan subscribers in the ZCode editor and Z.ai Chat (model name glm-5.4, and glm-5.4[1m] in Claude Code). It's also available via partner tools (Trae, WorkBuddy, Qoder).</p>

<p>On price the model is 33% cheaper on the Coding Plan than its predecessor on the Coding Plan, with an output credit multiplier of 16 against 24 for GLM-5.3 and the plan itself starts at $18/month. Z.ai states the model serves at up to 200 tokens per second off peak and 70-80 tokens per second at peak. V2, V3 and team plan subscribers all received a 50% increase in their usage allowance. API access will open following a safety review and open weights are planned following safety hardening (no date for either of those).</p>

<h2>What the Zhipu GLM-5.4 release means</h2>

<p>The Loop Transformer is a very elegant solution to the scaling problem. Rather than train ever bigger models (which is more compute and more memory), Z.ai simply reuses the same layer stack a second time, buying in depth rather than in parameters. If it works, that points to a future where frontier class intelligence comes in smaller, cheaper packages which is obviously good for everyone running models on constrained hardware. The 33% credit saving in the commercial version is the commercial manifestation of that same principle: same work, less money.</p>

<p>The catch is verification. There's been no full technical introduction from Z.ai and no benchmark table has been published at the time of the rollout so the two to three trillion parameter claim is entirely Z.ai's word for now. They do point out one limitation in that the model can get into very long thinking loops, though they do say the added self correction does improve accuracy. Until independent benchmarks and the promised open weights come along the Loop Transformer is a very promising architecture with an unproven headline. It's entering a very fast moving field (we've covered the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/google-gemini-4-neon-launch-20261121">Google Gemini 4 Neon launch</a> in our news story) and there's more on the wider field in our <a href="https://growentix.cloud/tech-ai">Tech news</a>.</p>

<h2>What the sources do not say</h2>

<p>No benchmark table for the flagship model has been published, so the claim of matching two-to-three-trillion-parameter models cannot be checked independently. The sources do not say when the API will open, when open weights will be released, or what the per-token API price will be. Z.ai has not stated the training cost, the training data, or how the loop design affects latency on long tasks. Some secondary coverage cites benchmark scores for smaller GLM-5.4 variants. Still, those figures describe different model sizes and aren't used here because no benchmark table for the 750-billion-parameter flagship has been published.</p>

